%% ABSTRACT-BEGIN
\begin{abstract}
Optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs) worn on the scalp sit closer to the brain than the superconducting quantum interference devices (SQUIDs) of conventional magnetoencephalography (MEG) and record larger fields. Their sensor noise is higher, however, and they also pick up more of the brain's background activity, so their advantage depends on noise as well as signal. Spherical-head analyses have compared point sensors with one assumed ratio of OPM to SQUID noise, predicting the largest benefit for superficial sources and small heads. Yet a child's head also sits far from the sensors of an adult helmet. Here, we compared the << F.g2_squid_channels >>-channel Neuromag system with a dense array of << F.g2_dense_sites >> single-axis OPMs and with OPMs at << F.g2_matched_sites >> of its << F.g2_squid_sites >> sensor sites, using matched-filter detectability with explicit sensor, brain and room noise. We modeled an adult head, << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled copies and << F.wr_n_principal_templates_words >> infant templates, each in the adult helmet and in a helmet fitted at the adult's gap, checked the noise model against Neuromag's measured noise, and simulated interictal spikes. In the adult, brain noise reversed the comparison: at an OPM noise of << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz, the dense array had << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> times Neuromag's detectability (<< F.g2_ci_level_pct >> parcel-bootstrap interval << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>), whereas the OPMs at the Neuromag sites were on a par. The OPM noise floor and array design determined which system had the higher detectability, and the Neuromag channels and signal-to-noise measure determined which system a reported value favored. The dense advantage, however, was as large as the model's error for Neuromag's noise. With Neuromag's measured noise covariance, the dense array's ratio fell to << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ci >>, and its break-even OPM noise would fall from << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd >> to near << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz. In the smaller heads, the adult helmet cost Neuromag more detectability than in the adult, and the OPM advantage exceeded the adult's by << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB. A helmet fitted at the adult's gap removed most of this excess when each head kept its own dense array (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB remained). Under the modeled noise and at a nominal false-event rate, idealized detectors found superficial simulated spikes with the dense array at << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower dipole strength than with Neuromag. Overall, these results suggest that proximity alone does not set the on-scalp advantage: array design, sensor noise and the fit of the SQUID helmet determine whether OPMs outperform SQUIDs, which matters when OPM and SQUID recordings are compared in children and in epilepsy.
\end{abstract}
