\section{Materials and methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Study design and estimands}\label{sec:design}

To compare arrays without committing to a particular detector, we used the known-topography detectability of each source. This is the output SNR of the matched filter that knows the field pattern of the source and the covariance of the noise,
\begin{equation}
d = \sqrt{\symbf{s}^{\top}\symbf{C}^{-1}\symbf{s}}.
\label{eq:d}
\end{equation}
Here, $\symbf{s}$ is the noise-free field of a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm cortical dipole at the channels of the array, and $\symbf{C}$ is the noise covariance of the array (Section~\ref{sec:noise}), taken as known (the oracle covariance). Two arrays were compared target by target, by the ratio $d_{\mathrm{OPM}}/d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}$ or, in decibels, by
\begin{equation}
D = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{OPM}}}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}},
\label{eq:D}
\end{equation}
which is positive when the OPM array has the higher detectability.

Each comparison was summarized over the cortex. For the adult, we took the unweighted median of the target-wise ratios over all << F.g2_n_targets >> targets, including the << F.g2_medialwall_n >> vertices on the medial wall. This convention was set for the adult before the smaller heads were added, and Table~\ref{tab:estimands} relates it to theirs. Excluding these vertices would change the median ratio of the dense array to << F.dp("wr_g2_dense_ib_ratio_no_medial_wall", 2) >>. Because the smaller heads differ in cortical area, we summarized each of them by the area-weighted median of $D$ over its cortical targets, medial wall excluded. To compare a smaller head with the adult, we placed both in the same helmet and pose (Section~\ref{sec:arrays}) and computed the change from the adult, $\Delta$, from paired differences rather than as the difference of their medians. For the scaled adults, which share the adult's vertices, $\Delta$ was computed as the area-weighted median of $D_{\mathrm{head}}(v) - D_{\mathrm{adult}}(v)$ over common vertices. For the other heads, the area-weighted median of $D$ was taken within every atlas parcel that holds at least << F.g3b_cfg_min_n >> targets in both heads, and $\Delta$ was computed as the median of the parcel differences, weighted by the adult's parcel areas.

\subsection{Head models}\label{sec:heads}

We used the MNE sample subject as the adult head \citep{gramfort2013}, with its FreeSurfer surfaces and atlas labels \citep{desikan2006,fischl2012}. The vertices of an MNE-Python oct-6 source space on the white-matter surface were used as targets, each a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm dipole normal to the cortex. We kept only vertices at least << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm inside the inner skull. The depth of a target was defined as its distance to the nearest point of the MRI scalp. Forward fields were computed with a three-layer boundary-element model (BEM; \citealp{hamalainen1989}) with conductivities of << F.g2_conductivity >>~S/m, whose outer surface was the MRI scalp. The BEM was solved by linear collocation, with << F.g2_head_triangles_coarse >> triangles per skull surface and the scalp refined to << F.g2_head_triangles >> triangles. Compared with an exact three-shell sphere, the field of this BEM had a 95th-percentile error of << F.g2_bemsphere_20480_h2mm_p95_pct >> at 2~mm and << F.g2_bemsphere_20480_h15mm_p95_pct >> at 15~mm outside the outer surface. When the scalp was refined from << F.g2_head_triangles_coarse >> to << F.g2_head_triangles >> triangles, the ratio of the dense array changed from << F.g2_skin_bem3_5120_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> to << F.g2_skin_bem3_skin20480_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> on a subset of targets.

Of the << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}), four carry the main analysis, and we call them the principal smaller heads. << F.wr_n_scaled_words|capitalize >> of them were made by scaling the adult to school-age size and to the head circumference of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template. These scaled adults keep the adult anatomy exactly and are the main test of head size and helmet fit. The other << F.wr_n_principal_templates_words >> are the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >>-month infant templates, averages of many MRIs \citep{oreilly2021,richards2016}, which add infant head shapes, with the caveat about their scalp convention given below. Because all of the anatomies share the noise calibration, the adult's conductivities and the processing, none of them is an independent replication.

The remaining << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads are reported separately in Section~S10. The << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template was classed as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >> by an MRI check, because its white surface fitted its T1-weighted image best after a << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>-mm shift, compared with << F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >>~mm in the other two templates. The MRIs of << F.wr_n_children_words >> typically developing school-aged children, A, B and C (<< F.g3b_children_age_range >> years), were taken from OpenNeuro dataset ds005234 \citep{fadeev2024,fadeev2025}. Their anatomy was prepared in three further steps (Section~S4). In the dataset, the inner skull of these children lay a median of << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >>~mm below the scalp over the upper head. We therefore moved it inward, toward << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_depth >>~mm below the scalp, while keeping it at least << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_min_cortex >>~mm from the white-surface vertices. We transferred their fiducials from the adult by a similarity transform fitted between the white surfaces and checked their surfaces against their T1-weighted MRIs (Fig.~S6). The surfaces were found to be registered to the images, but the check cannot verify the cortex within a few millimeters of the scalp. Because the << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>-mm limit on targets also left out << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of the children's cortex within 8~mm of the scalp, their results do not describe a near-scalp cortex.

We made two further checks of the principal heads. First, the scalps of the infant templates lie << F.wr_qc_principal_templates_offset_outside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm outside their own MRI head boundaries, whereas the adult's scalp lies << F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >>~mm inside, a difference of << F.wr_qc_principal_template_adult_convention_mm_range >>~mm between the conventions (Section~S4.3). Second, replacing the three-layer BEM with a single-compartment BEM, which has no skull or scalp conductivity layer, changed the difference between the median $D$ of each principal head and that of the adult by << F.meth_principal_bem1_change_db_range >>~dB. This check tests the conductivity layers, not the position of the scalp or the inner skull.

