\subsection{Sensor arrays and helmets}\label{sec:arrays}

We modeled the Neuromag system (<< F.g2_squid_channels >> channels; ``Neuromag'' below) with the sensor geometry of the sample recording and the T3 coil definitions of MNE-Python (types << F.meth_coil_grad_type >> and << F.meth_coil_mag_type >>). Each gradiometer was integrated at << F.meth_coil_grad_points >> points and each magnetometer at << F.meth_coil_mag_points >>. Neuromag's white noise was set to the typical value of the TRIUX datasheet, << F.meth_squid_mag_asd >>~fT/√Hz for the magnetometers and << F.meth_squid_grad_asd >>~fT/(cm~√Hz) for the gradiometers \citep{elekta_triux}. Its coils lay << F.meth_dewar_mm >>~mm outward from the inner surface of the helmet (the Dewar spacing), and no signal-space separation was applied. We compared each OPM array with all << F.g2_squid_channels >> Neuromag channels and, as alternative comparators, with the << F.g2_squid_grad_channels >> gradiometers or the << F.g2_squid_sites >> magnetometers alone. Besides detectability, we computed two sensor-level measures, the peak-channel SNR and the mean-power SNR \citep{goldenholz2009}, which are defined in Table~\ref{tab:estimands}.

Each OPM was modeled as a single-axis sensor whose sensing center lay nominally << F.g2_dense_standoff_mm >>~mm above the MRI scalp \citep{alem2023,osborne2018}, and a few sites were moved outward for clearance. The field of each OPM was averaged over a << F.g2_opm_cell_mm >>-mm cube with a << F.meth_opm_cell_points >>-point Gauss--Legendre rule, a modeling choice that changes the peak field by at most << F.wr_opm_cell_peak_effect_pct >>. The sensitive axis was taken as the head-surface normal, averaged within << F.meth_opm_axis_radius_mm >>~mm of the site. Nearly radial axes would give the dense array a ratio of << F.dp("meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> instead of << F.dp("meth_hse_final_dense_proj_ratio_3dp", 2) >> with the room field projected out (Sections~\ref{sec:noise} and S3). We set the OPM white noise to << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz in the primary analysis, flat over << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz, and varied it over << F.wr_opm_asd_sweep_list >>~fT/√Hz.

The OPMs were placed on the scalp above the plane through the preauricular points and a point << F.wr_cover_brow_offset_mm >>~mm above the nasion, at least << F.g2_preauricular_exclusion_mm >>~mm from either preauricular point. The dense array was packed into this region at a minimum spacing of << F.g2_dense_packing_mm >>~mm, which gave << F.g2_dense_sites >> sites on the adult, more than any clinical OPM array in the studies of Table~S19. For the site-matched array, one OPM was placed at each Neuromag site whose projection onto the scalp fell inside the region, << F.g2_matched_sites >> of the << F.g2_squid_sites >> sites (Fig.~\ref{fig:arrays}). Clinical systems reach about 100--128 channels with dual- or triaxial sensors at fewer sites \citep{feys2025,shen2026}. We did not model such arrays, and neither single-axis array represents one, because both differ from clinical arrays in site count and sensing axes. To test the effect of extra axes, we added a triaxial array at the site-matched sites (<< F.g2_triax_channels >> channels).

In the fixed adult helmet, the adult's sensors and noise were kept for every head (Fig.~\ref{fig:helmets}). For the primary placement in this helmet, which we call top contact, each head was first posed with its head coordinate frame where the adult's had been recorded. It was then raised until the nearest magnetometer coil was << F.g3b_cfg_clearance >>~mm from the scalp, a best-fit assumption that favors Neuromag \citep{gaetz2008,tesan2010}. To describe the effect of placement, we also used a family of << F.g3b_n_family_placements >> source-blind placements, chosen from the geometry alone (contacts, shifts and rotations; Section~S5). The dense array was rebuilt on every smaller head by the same coverage and spacing rules, and the site-matched array took the Neuromag sites of that head at top contact (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}).

To separate head size from helmet fit, we also placed each head in a helmet fitted at the adult's gap. The head was moved sideways from the recorded pose onto the midline of the helmet, without being raised to contact (the laterally centered pose). The helmet was then scaled about the head origin until the median magnetometer-to-scalp gap equaled that of the adult in the same pose, << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >>~mm. For comparison, the adult's gap is << F.g2_squid_scalp_dist_median_mm >>~mm at its measured head position and << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_gap_mm >>~mm at top contact. No coil was allowed within the << F.g3b_cfg_dewar_spacing >>-mm Dewar spacing of the scalp, a limit that was not reached in any principal head. The fitted helmet keeps the adult's sensors and does not model a pediatric SQUID system.

Our primary measure of helmet fit, which we call the interaction, is the paired change, from the adult to a smaller head, in how much detectability Neuromag loses in the fixed helmet relative to the fitted one. For each target, the contrast between the two helmets within one head is
\begin{equation}
F(v) = D_{\mathrm{fixed}}(v) - D_{\mathrm{fitted}}(v) = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag,\,fitted}}(v)}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag,\,fixed}}(v)},
\label{eq:F}
\end{equation}
in which the detectability of the OPM cancels. The interaction of a smaller head is obtained by applying the $\Delta$ estimator of Section~\ref{sec:design} to $F$ instead of $D$. This is the area-weighted median of $F_{\mathrm{head}}(v) - F_{\mathrm{adult}}(v)$ over common vertices for the scaled adults, and the median of the parcel differences, weighted by the adult's parcel areas, for the templates. As a median of paired differences, it equals neither the difference of the two median contrasts nor $\Delta_{\mathrm{fixed}} - \Delta_{\mathrm{fitted}}$, and Table~S6 gives the non-additivity residual of each head. The interaction does not depend on the assumed OPM noise, but it does depend on the modeled Neuromag noise and on the anatomy.

We used three secondary controls of the construction (Section~S5). One helmet was fitted at the adult's top-contact gap, << F.rev_cgap_adult_top_gap_mm >>~mm. Another was scaled with the head, which scales the gap too. In the third, the fitted helmet was kept but the adult's dense array was subsampled to the number of sites of each smaller head, to compare the heads at equal OPM site count.

