\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Determinants of the adult comparison}\label{sec:res-adult}

Brain noise reversed the comparison between the dense array and Neuromag (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). When only sensor noise was modeled, Neuromag had a higher detectability than either OPM array (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}). When brain noise was added, the dense array had the higher detectability at << F.wr_g2_ahead_n >> of the << F.g2_n_targets >> targets, with a median ratio to Neuromag of << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. In contrast, the site-matched array was on a par with Neuromag (<< F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci >>). This pattern was kept when the room field was projected out, although the site-matched array moved slightly below parity.

Brain noise dominated the noise of both systems (Section~S3). When the room field was included, brain noise made up << F.g2_noise_share_dense_brain_pct >> of the in-band variance of an OPM channel in the dense array and << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_brain_pct >> of that of a Neuromag magnetometer. The room field made up most of the rest of the magnetometer variance, << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_room_pct >>. As a result, brain noise raised the noise of a Neuromag magnetometer about << F.g2_noise_inflation_squid_mag_1dp >>-fold over its sensor floor, but that of an OPM only about << F.g2_noise_inflation_dense_1dp >>-fold. Thus, the higher sensor noise of the OPM mattered less once brain noise was present.

The ratio also depended on which Neuromag channels served as the comparator and on the SNR measure (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). The dense array had a larger advantage against the gradiometers alone than against the magnetometers alone (Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Peak-channel SNR is the closest analog of the sensor-level SNRs of clinical studies (Section~S1). By this measure, the dense array had the higher SNR when compared with the magnetometers, but Neuromag had the higher SNR when its best channel of all << F.g2_squid_channels >> was used. Only small advantages were found with mean-power SNR, for both OPM arrays (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}). Which system a reported number favors thus depends on the Neuromag channels and the SNR measure it uses.

Among the model variants, the OPM noise floor had the largest effect (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). As the OPM noise rose over the sweep, the ratio of the dense array fell to parity and that of the site-matched array fell below it (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D). The break-even OPM noise, at which the ratio equals 1 under the modeled noise, was << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz for the dense array (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, diamonds). The site-matched array broke even at << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_ib_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_ib_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz, an interval that includes the primary level. With the room field projected out, the break-even levels fell to << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_proj_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_proj_asd_ci >> and << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_proj_asd >> << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_proj_asd_ci >>~fT/√Hz (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, open diamonds). All of these levels assume the modeled Neuromag noise, which the next section compares with the measured noise.

The other model variants moved the ratio of the dense array less over the ranges tested (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). An additional gap between OPM and scalp lowered the ratio but did not reverse it, whereas combining << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz with an additional << F.g2_opm_gap_small_mm >>-mm gap did reverse it (<< F.g2_joint_gap3mm_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_joint_gap3mm_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}). A triaxial array at the site-matched sites recovered most of the dense advantage, and still recovered part of it when its tangential axes were twice as noisy (Fig.~\ref{fig:conditions}, triangles). The ratio changed little when the covariance was estimated from a finite number of simulated samples, as many as a minute of data would hold, or when a 3--70-Hz band was used. Thus, besides brain noise, the array and the OPM noise floor determined which system had the higher detectability in the adult, and the comparator and the SNR measure determined which system a reported SNR favored.

