\subsection{The noise model against measured Neuromag noise}\label{sec:res-noise}

When we compared the model with Neuromag's measured noise, it reproduced some properties and missed others (Figs.~S2 and S3). It predicted << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the measured brain-noise amplitude of the magnetometers, a quantity not used in its calibration, and << F.rev_cov_heart_mag_amp_without_ratio >> with the field of the heart removed. The gradiometer level at randomly held-out sites agreed with the measurement nearly by construction (<< F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >>, with a << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> range of << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >> over << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> random splits; Fig.~S3B). However, the modeled brain noise was too flat from inferior to superior sites. Calibrated on the inferior half of the sites, it predicted << F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >> times the measured gradiometer amplitude of the superior half. Calibrated on the superior half, it predicted << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >> times that of the inferior half. The model also predicted the per-channel variances poorly, and its magnetometer correlations fell off with distance faster than the measured ones.

Thus, the measured covariance differed from the modeled one in structure as well as in level. The positive part of the difference between the measured and modeled magnetometer brain-noise covariances, that is, the excess of the measured brain noise over the model, summed to << F.rev_cov_excess_brain_mag_pos_over_model_ratio >> times the summed variance of the modeled brain noise. Sampling error alone would give << F.rev_cov_excess_brain_mag_pos_over_model_null_range >>, and the excess was concentrated in a few far-field components (Section~S3). When its measured covariance was used, Neuromag had << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ci >> times its modeled detectability (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}A), a factor as large as the whole advantage of the dense array. This factor varied by lobe, from << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_temporal_corrected_ratio >> in the temporal lobe to << F.rev_cov_det_lobe_cingulate_corrected_ratio >> in the cingulate. The measured covariance thus held more brain-noise variance than the model and yet gave Neuromag a higher detectability. Detectability depends only on the noise along the field pattern of each source after whitening. The measured noise must therefore be lower than the model along the cortical field patterns, with its excess in directions that the whitening down-weights.

What this implies for the OPM depends on its real noise, which no measurement here constrains (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Scenarios A, B and E left both modeled ratios nearly unchanged, whereas scenario C, the least favorable to the OPM, lowered both below parity. Under scenario D, the ratio of the dense array fell to << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ci >>, an interval that includes 1 (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B). The ratio of the site-matched array fell to << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ci >>, below parity. Under scenario D, neither OPM array therefore had a clear advantage over Neuromag at the primary OPM noise.

Scenario D also moves the break-even levels. Because Neuromag's measured detectability does not change with the OPM noise, the ratio under scenario D still falls as the OPM noise rises, starting from << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> for the dense array and << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> for the site-matched array at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz. The break-even level of the dense array would then lie near << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz rather than at << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd >>~fT/√Hz: above it at the point estimate, although the interval of the ratio includes 1. It would lie below << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz if its ratio fell between these levels as the modeled ratio does, from << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> to << F.g2_sens_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> (Table~\ref{tab:adult}). The modeled ratio of the site-matched array rises only from << F.g2_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> to << F.g2_sens_asd7ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> between << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >> and << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_min >>~fT/√Hz, so by the same reasoning this array would not break even within the sweep.

The omissions we tested moved the advantage little at the primary OPM noise (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C; Table~\ref{tab:adult}). The ratio of the dense array was unchanged when the near-skull cortex was added, as background or as targets (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C). Colored OPM noise with the highest corner frequency, << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>~Hz, lowered it slightly, under the primary detector and under one that also whitens across frequency. Similarly, adding cardiac and ocular sources changed the ratio by only << F.rev_ns_ff_dense_vs_combined_ib_change_pct_range >>.

The joint runs combined the near-skull background and the << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner under the frequency-whitening detector (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, dashed). With white OPM noise, this detector gave the dense array a ratio of << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz. The joint run lowered this ratio to << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> (Table~\ref{tab:adult}). Raising the white OPM noise alone to << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz lowered it more, to << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_white_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. In the joint run, the ratio fell to << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz and to << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D). When the cardiac and ocular sources were added, the ratio at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz was << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_all_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>. Under the same detector, the omitted noise sources thus lowered the ratio less than a rise of the OPM noise from << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >> to << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz did.

