\subsection{Depth and brain regions}\label{sec:res-depth}

The dense advantage was largest near the scalp and fell with depth (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A). The ratio of the dense array was << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ratio >> << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ci >> in the << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm bin. It had fallen to << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_25_30_ratio >> << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_25_30_ci >> by the << F.g2_depth_bin_25_30_mm >>~mm bin (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A). From the << F.g2_depth_bin_35_40_mm >> to the << F.g2_depth_bin_55_60_mm >>~mm bin, it stayed at << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_combined_ib_35_60_range >>, and every interval excluded parity. In contrast, the site-matched array had an equal-SNR depth, beyond which Neuromag had the higher detectability, even with sensor plus brain noise. Its intervals were above 1 down to the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_above1_through_bin >>~mm bin and below 1 from the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A).

