When the room field was projected out, the dense array also had an equal-SNR depth, and that of the site-matched array moved toward the scalp (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A, dashed). The bin median of the dense array then fell below 1 from the << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_proj_median_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin, and that of the site-matched array from the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_proj_median_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin. Intervals below 1 appeared only in the << F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_proj_bins_ci_below1 >>~mm bin for the dense array and from the << F.rev_db_matched_vs_combined_proj_ci_below1_from_bin >>~mm bin for the site-matched array.

The ratio of the dense array fell with depth whether or not brain noise was present. The fall was already present with sensor noise alone (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A, dotted) and was also seen in the peak field itself (Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}B). Adding brain noise multiplied the ratio by a nearly constant factor in every bin (<< F.g2_brainmult_dense_vs_combined_range >>). This suggests that the fall came from the source--sensor geometry rather than from the noise.

The dense array had the higher detectability almost everywhere on the cortex, most of all on the lateral convexity (Fig.~\ref{fig:maps}). These regional results are exploratory point estimates under the modeled noise. The largest dense advantage was found in the frontal lobe (<< F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_frontal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}A). It was smallest in the cingulate (<< F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_lobe_cingulate_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) and the insula (<< F.g2_lobe_insula_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}A). Of the regions chosen from the epilepsy literature, the precentral gyrus favored the dense array most (<< F.reg_precentral_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>) and the parahippocampal cortex least (<< F.reg_parahippocampal_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:regions}A). Another << F.reg_dk_dense_vs_combined_ib_below_parahippocampal_count >> atlas parcels, mostly cingulate and medial, had a smaller dense advantage than the parahippocampal cortex.

