\subsection{Smaller heads in the fixed adult helmet}\label{sec:res-heads}

When we placed the principal smaller heads in the fixed adult helmet, every one of them had a larger OPM advantage than the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:heads}; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). Their $D$ at top contact was << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_range >>~dB, compared with << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d >> << F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_ci >>~dB in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:heads}A). The paired change from the adult, $\Delta$, was << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB (Fig.~\ref{fig:heads}B). When the background variance per unit area was halved or doubled, the $D$ of every principal head still exceeded that of the adult, by << F.g3b_principal_bg0p5_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >> and << F.g3b_principal_bg2_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_range >>~dB. These heads also sat farther from the Neuromag sensors, with median magnetometer-to-scalp gaps of << F.g3b_principal_top_gap_range >>~mm, compared with << F.g3b_adult_top_gap >>~mm in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}A). In other words, the larger OPM advantage of these heads came with a larger gap.

