Placement, an uncertainty that the parcel intervals do not include, mattered as much as head size. Over the source-blind placements of the fixed helmet, the change from the adult in the school-age scaled adult spanned << F.rev_cgap_school_band_dense_vs_combined_ib_range >>~dB (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B). This range, computed from differences of medians, is wider than the parcel interval of the change at top contact, << F.g3b_school_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_ci >>~dB. The change stayed positive at every placement in each of the << F.wr_n_principal_words >> heads (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B, gray ranges), and the adult's own $D$ spanned << F.g3b_adult_family_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_span >>~dB over these placements. The << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads of Section~S10 had changes of << F.g3b_provisional_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, and the << F.wr_n_children_words >> children had the smallest, << F.g3b_children_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB. A larger OPM advantage in a smaller head may therefore reflect the fit of the adult helmet as much as the head itself.

\subsection{Helmet fit versus head size}\label{sec:res-fit}

Relative to the fitted helmet, the fixed helmet cost Neuromag more detectability in every principal smaller head than in the adult (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}C; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). This interaction was << F.wr_cgap_principal_interaction_range >>~dB in the principal heads, with every interval above zero (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}C). The two scaled adults were at the extremes, with the templates in between at << F.wr_cgap_principal_templates_interaction_range >>~dB. In the << F.wr_n_provisional_words >> heads of Section~S10, the interaction was << F.wr_cgap_provisional_interaction_range >>~dB, and only the interval of child C included zero (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_combined_ib_interaction >> << F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_combined_ib_interaction_ci >>~dB). The interaction exceeded the contrasts of the principal heads between the two helmets (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib_range >>~dB), mainly because the adult's contrast was negative (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fitted_combined_ib >>~dB). The adult sits farther from the sensors in the fitted helmet (median gap << F.rev_cgap_adult_gap_mm >>~mm) than at top contact (<< F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_gap_mm >>~mm). About half of its contrast, however, comes from the construction itself: at an equal median gap, between top contact and a helmet fitted at its top-contact gap, the contrast of the adult was << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib >> << F.rev_cgap_adult_fixed_minus_fittedtop_combined_ib_ci >>~dB (Section~S5).

