When we fitted the helmet to each head and gave each head its own dense array, most of the extra OPM advantage of the smaller heads disappeared (Fig.~\ref{fig:fit}B; Table~\ref{tab:heads}). With both the smaller head and the adult in helmets fitted at the adult's gap, $\Delta$ was << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, with interval bounds of << F.wr_cgap_principal_fitted_delta_ci_bounds_range >>~dB (Table~\ref{tab:heads}). Every interval included zero except that of the school-age scaled adult, whose lower bound touched it. In a helmet fitted instead at the adult's top-contact gap, these heads kept a change of << F.wr_cgap_principal_fittedtop_vs_top_range >>~dB from the adult at top contact (Section~S5). When the room field was projected out, the change in the fitted helmet was negative in every principal head (<< F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_proj_delta_range >>~dB), and only << F.wr_cgap_principal_fitted_proj_delta_n_ci_includes_zero_words >> of the << F.wr_n_principal_words >> intervals included zero (Table~S6). In the fitted helmet, the smaller heads thus had no extra advantage over the adult, and a small disadvantage once the room field was projected out.

Because the fitted helmet changes the gap, the placement and the coverage of the sensors together, none of these quantities apportions the effects exactly. At equal OPM site count, the change in the fitted helmet rose to << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_eqsites_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB, compared with << F.rev_cgap_principal_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB with the array of each head and << F.g3b_principal_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB in the fixed helmet (Table~\ref{tab:heads}). Thus, the fitted helmet removed most of the extra advantage only because each smaller head kept its own, smaller dense array. This comparison suggests that the smaller heads kept a head-size gain of a few tenths of a decibel in the fitted helmet, which their fewer OPM sites offset.

\subsection{Simulated interictal spikes}\label{sec:res-spikes}

The dense array detected simulated superficial spikes at a lower strength than Neuromag in the adult and in every principal smaller head (Fig.~\ref{fig:confirm}A; Table~\ref{tab:spikes}). This held under the modeled noise, for idealized detectors and at a nominal false-event rate. The smaller heads were placed at top contact in the fixed adult helmet, whose fit raises their modeled OPM advantage (Section~\ref{sec:res-fit}), and spikes were not simulated in the fitted helmet. The S50 ratio Neuromag/OPM, the declared endpoint of the pre-specified run, was << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_ratio_range >>, and every location-bootstrap interval was above 1 (Table~\ref{tab:spikes}). Spikes << F.cf_band >>~mm deep were thus detected at << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower strength with the dense array. As expected when detectability is higher, the declared sign-flip test favored the dense array in each of these anatomies (Holm-adjusted p << F.cf_adult_principal_dense_vs_combined_practical_p_holm_max|replace("<", "< ") >> over the << F.meth_n_anatomies|words >> declared anatomies; Table~S11). This test checks the transfer from detectability to detection under the model. In the adult, the S50 ratio (<< F.cf_adult_dense_vs_combined_practical_ratio >>) was of the order of the detectability ratio at these depths (<< F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_15_20_ratio >>--<< F.rev_db_dense_vs_combined_ib_10_15_ratio >>; Fig.~\ref{fig:depth}A), so the detectability advantage carried over to the detection of these spikes.

